WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.220
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:12.220 --> 00:12.720
JUSTIN: OK.

00:12.720 --> 00:15.360
So it's a pleasure to
see all of you guys.

00:15.360 --> 00:16.450
I'm Justin.

00:16.450 --> 00:20.700
I'm your third
instructor for 6.006.

00:20.700 --> 00:22.540
This is my first time
with this course.

00:22.540 --> 00:24.415
Although, of course,
this is material that we

00:24.415 --> 00:27.660
all know and love in the
computer science department.

00:27.660 --> 00:29.880
I'll admit, I find the
prospect of teaching

00:29.880 --> 00:32.910
sorting to 400 people
all at once is mildly,

00:32.910 --> 00:33.960
low key terrifying.

00:33.960 --> 00:35.670
But we're going
to give it a shot.

00:35.670 --> 00:38.520
And hopefully, that will subside
as the lecture goes on today,

00:38.520 --> 00:40.080
all right?

00:40.080 --> 00:43.140
So we're going to pick up where
we left off in our last lecture

00:43.140 --> 00:45.300
and continue on
with a similar theme

00:45.300 --> 00:47.700
that we're going to see
throughout our algorithms class

00:47.700 --> 00:49.050
here in 6.006.

00:49.050 --> 00:51.480
I think Jason and
colleagues have

00:51.480 --> 00:54.180
done a really great job
of organizing this class

00:54.180 --> 00:55.890
around some interesting themes.

00:55.890 --> 00:58.650
And so I thought I'd start
with just a tiny bit of review

00:58.650 --> 01:00.780
of some key vocabulary words.

01:00.780 --> 01:04.030
Incidentally, typically, I
teach the intro graphics class,

01:04.030 --> 01:04.980
the geometry course.

01:04.980 --> 01:07.020
And last year, I
got feedback that

01:07.020 --> 01:08.850
said I have serial
killer handwriting.

01:08.850 --> 01:11.620
I'm not 100% sure
what that means.

01:11.620 --> 01:13.890
But we're going to use
the slides a tiny bit more

01:13.890 --> 01:15.990
than normal, just to make
sure you guys can read.

01:15.990 --> 01:17.407
And when I'm writing
on the board,

01:17.407 --> 01:19.920
at any point, if you
can't tell what I wrote,

01:19.920 --> 01:21.720
it's definitely me and not you.

01:21.720 --> 01:23.590
So just let me know.

01:23.590 --> 01:28.485
But in any event, in 6.006, all
the way back in our lecture 1--

01:28.485 --> 01:29.860
I know that was
a long time ago--

01:29.860 --> 01:34.080
we introduced two big keywords
that are closely related,

01:34.080 --> 01:35.460
but not precisely the same.

01:35.460 --> 01:37.450
Hopefully, I've
gotten this right.

01:37.450 --> 01:39.610
But roughly,
there's a theme here

01:39.610 --> 01:42.960
which is that there's an object
called an interface, which is

01:42.960 --> 01:44.795
just a program specification.

01:44.795 --> 01:46.170
It's just telling
us that there's

01:46.170 --> 01:48.940
a collection of operations
that we want to implement.

01:48.940 --> 01:51.630
So for example, a set, as
we're going to see today,

01:51.630 --> 01:53.550
is like a big pile of things.

01:53.550 --> 01:56.550
And behind the scenes,
how I choose to implement

01:56.550 --> 02:01.100
it can affect the runtime
and how efficient my set is.

02:01.100 --> 02:02.850
But the actual way
that I interact with it

02:02.850 --> 02:06.060
is the same, whether I use an
unsorted array, a sorted array,

02:06.060 --> 02:07.380
what have you.

02:07.380 --> 02:10.350
On the other hand, what
happens behind the scenes

02:10.350 --> 02:12.430
is something called
a data structure,

02:12.430 --> 02:14.670
which is a way to
actually, in some sense,

02:14.670 --> 02:15.900
implement an interface.

02:15.900 --> 02:17.940
So this is the object
that on my computer

02:17.940 --> 02:19.830
is actually storing
the information

02:19.830 --> 02:22.260
and implementing the
set of operations

02:22.260 --> 02:24.970
that I've laid out
in my interface.

02:24.970 --> 02:26.820
And so this sort of
distinction, I think,

02:26.820 --> 02:29.370
is a critical theme
in this course

02:29.370 --> 02:31.412
because, for instance, in
the first couple weeks,

02:31.412 --> 02:33.828
we're going to talk about many
different ways to implement

02:33.828 --> 02:34.350
a set.

02:34.350 --> 02:35.970
I'm going to see that
there's a bunch of tradeoffs.

02:35.970 --> 02:38.130
Some of them are really
fast for certain operations

02:38.130 --> 02:39.790
and slow for others.

02:39.790 --> 02:42.660
And so essentially, we have
two different decisions

02:42.660 --> 02:44.370
to make when we
choose an algorithm.

02:44.370 --> 02:46.320
One is making sure
that the interface

02:46.320 --> 02:48.540
is correct for the problem
that we're concerned with.

02:48.540 --> 02:51.390
And the other is choosing an
appropriate data structure

02:51.390 --> 02:55.548
whose efficiency, and memory
usage, and so on aligns

02:55.548 --> 02:57.840
with the priorities that we
have for the application we

02:57.840 --> 02:58.990
have in mind.

02:58.990 --> 03:01.270
So hopefully, this high
level theme makes sense.

03:01.270 --> 03:04.380
And really, spiritually, I
think this is the main message

03:04.380 --> 03:06.700
to get out of this course in
the first couple of weeks,

03:06.700 --> 03:08.250
even though these
O's, and thetas,

03:08.250 --> 03:12.660
and so on are easy to lose
the forest through the trees.

03:12.660 --> 03:15.730
In any event, today,
in our lecture,

03:15.730 --> 03:17.793
we're concerned with one
particular interface,

03:17.793 --> 03:18.710
which is called a set.

03:18.710 --> 03:20.680
A set is exactly
what it sounds like.

03:20.680 --> 03:22.840
It's a big pile of things.

03:22.840 --> 03:26.648
And so a set interface
is like an object

03:26.648 --> 03:28.440
that just you can keep
adding things to it.

03:28.440 --> 03:31.890
And then querying inside of
my set, is this object here?

03:31.890 --> 03:32.730
Can I find it?

03:32.730 --> 03:35.110
And then maybe I
associate with my objects

03:35.110 --> 03:36.610
in my set different information.

03:36.610 --> 03:38.867
So for example,
maybe I have a set

03:38.867 --> 03:40.950
which represents all the
students in our classroom

03:40.950 --> 03:42.300
today.

03:42.300 --> 03:45.540
Yeah, and all of you guys are
associated with your student

03:45.540 --> 03:48.480
ID, which I believe
at MIT is a number,

03:48.480 --> 03:50.430
which has less than sign,
which is convenient.

03:50.430 --> 03:52.320
So we can sort all of you guys.

03:52.320 --> 03:54.180
And that might be the
key that's associated

03:54.180 --> 03:56.095
to every object in the room.

03:56.095 --> 03:57.720
And so when I'm
searching for students,

03:57.720 --> 03:59.970
maybe I enter in
the student number.

03:59.970 --> 04:02.280
And then I want to ask
my set, does this number

04:02.280 --> 04:05.700
exist in the set of
students that are in 6.006?

04:05.700 --> 04:07.890
And if it does, then I can
pull that student back.

04:07.890 --> 04:09.600
And then associated
with that object

04:09.600 --> 04:12.183
is a bunch of other information
that I'm not using to search--

04:12.183 --> 04:13.908
so for instance,
your name, your--

04:13.908 --> 04:16.200
I don't know-- your social
security number, your credit

04:16.200 --> 04:17.825
card number, all the
other stuff that I

04:17.825 --> 04:21.120
need to have a more
interesting profession.

04:21.120 --> 04:26.940
So in any event, let's fill
in the details of our set

04:26.940 --> 04:28.980
interface a little bit more.

04:28.980 --> 04:31.620
So our set is a container.

04:31.620 --> 04:34.710
It contains all of the
students in this classroom,

04:34.710 --> 04:37.060
in some virtual sense at least.

04:37.060 --> 04:40.150
And so to build up
our set, of course,

04:40.150 --> 04:43.260
we need an operation that
takes some iterable object A

04:43.260 --> 04:44.550
and builds a set out of it.

04:44.550 --> 04:47.040
So in other words, I
have all the students

04:47.040 --> 04:49.560
in this classroom represented
maybe in some other fashion.

04:49.560 --> 04:52.050
And I have to insert
them all into my set.

04:52.050 --> 04:54.510
I can also ask my set for
how much stuff is in it.

04:54.510 --> 04:56.190
Personally, I would
call that size.

04:56.190 --> 04:58.272
But length is cool, too.

04:58.272 --> 05:00.480
And then of course, there
are a lot of different ways

05:00.480 --> 05:02.590
that we can interact
with our set.

05:02.590 --> 05:08.400
So for instance, we could say,
is this student taking 6.006?

05:08.400 --> 05:10.830
So in set language, one
way to understand that

05:10.830 --> 05:13.460
is to say that the key--

05:13.460 --> 05:16.490
each person in this classroom
is associated with a key.

05:16.490 --> 05:19.460
Does that key k exist in my set?

05:19.460 --> 05:22.290
In which case, I'll
call this find function,

05:22.290 --> 05:26.420
which will give me back the
item with key k or maybe null

05:26.420 --> 05:29.660
or something if
it doesn't exist.

05:29.660 --> 05:33.770
Maybe I can delete an object
from my set or insert it.

05:33.770 --> 05:36.120
Notice that these are
dynamic operations,

05:36.120 --> 05:39.690
meaning that they actually
edit what's inside of my set.

05:39.690 --> 05:42.860
And then finally, there are all
kinds of different operations

05:42.860 --> 05:45.320
that I might want to do to
interact with my set beyond

05:45.320 --> 05:46.840
is this thing inside of it.

05:46.840 --> 05:51.680
So for instance, so for
the student ID example,

05:51.680 --> 05:54.440
probably finding the
minimum ID number in a class

05:54.440 --> 05:56.000
isn't a terribly
exciting exercise.

05:56.000 --> 05:57.917
But maybe I'm trying to
find the student who's

05:57.917 --> 05:59.000
been at MIT the longest.

05:59.000 --> 06:01.245
And so that would be a
reasonable heuristic.

06:01.245 --> 06:03.920
I actually have no idea
whether MIT student IDs

06:03.920 --> 06:05.310
are assigned linearly or not.

06:05.310 --> 06:09.680
But in any event, I could find
the smallest key, the largest

06:09.680 --> 06:11.480
key, and so on in my set.

06:11.480 --> 06:13.760
And these are all
reasonable operations

06:13.760 --> 06:16.040
to query, where
my object is just

06:16.040 --> 06:20.330
a thing that stores a lot of
different entities inside it.

06:20.330 --> 06:22.490
Now, this description here--

06:22.490 --> 06:25.130
notice that I've labeled
this as a set interface.

06:25.130 --> 06:28.270
This is not a set
data structure.

06:28.270 --> 06:31.270
And the way to remember that
is that I haven't told you how

06:31.270 --> 06:32.920
I've actually implemented this.

06:32.920 --> 06:35.630
I haven't told you that I'm
going to behind the scenes

06:35.630 --> 06:38.380
have an array of information,
and look inside of it,

06:38.380 --> 06:41.110
and that's how I'm going to
implement find min or find max

06:41.110 --> 06:42.360
with a for loop or whatever.

06:42.360 --> 06:44.500
All I'm telling you is
that a set is a thing that

06:44.500 --> 06:46.000
implements these operations.

06:46.000 --> 06:48.640
And behind the scenes, my
computer does what it does.

06:48.640 --> 06:50.200
Now, it might sound abstract.

06:50.200 --> 06:51.783
But it's more or
less what you guys do

06:51.783 --> 06:53.240
when you write code in Python.

06:53.240 --> 06:56.110
I think in Python
what we're calling

06:56.110 --> 06:57.880
a set is maybe a dictionary.

06:57.880 --> 06:59.350
I'm a Matlab Coder.

06:59.350 --> 07:00.040
I'm sorry.

07:00.040 --> 07:02.020
I'm a numerical
analysis kind of guy.

07:02.020 --> 07:06.250
But essentially, one
of the beautiful things

07:06.250 --> 07:08.800
about coding in these high
level programming languages

07:08.800 --> 07:11.410
is that they take care
of these ugly details.

07:11.410 --> 07:12.910
And what you're
left with is just

07:12.910 --> 07:16.420
the high level interfacing
with this object

07:16.420 --> 07:18.790
that you need at
the end of the day.

07:18.790 --> 07:21.550
So of course, in today's
lecture, now that we set out

07:21.550 --> 07:23.740
our goal, which is to fill in--

07:23.740 --> 07:27.258
if I wanted to write code
for a set, how could I do it?

07:27.258 --> 07:29.800
Now, of course, our goal is to
give different data structures

07:29.800 --> 07:31.717
that implement these,
and then understand them

07:31.717 --> 07:34.750
in terms of their efficiency,
data storage, correctness,

07:34.750 --> 07:36.830
all that good stuff.

07:36.830 --> 07:39.657
So before we get into
all these ugly details,

07:39.657 --> 07:40.740
let me pause for a second.

07:40.740 --> 07:44.307
Are there any questions
about this basic interface?

07:44.307 --> 07:46.140
You all should feel
free to stop me any time

07:46.140 --> 07:48.390
because this is going to be
hella boring if you're not

07:48.390 --> 07:51.050
getting the first slide or two.

07:51.050 --> 07:52.304
Yes?

07:52.304 --> 07:54.096
AUDIENCE: Can you
explain how [INAUDIBLE]..

08:01.080 --> 08:02.630
JUSTIN: That's a good question.

08:02.630 --> 08:04.630
So the question
was, what exactly

08:04.630 --> 08:06.270
is this insert operation doing?

08:06.270 --> 08:07.900
That's why working
on the analogy

08:07.900 --> 08:10.900
of the students in this
classroom is a reasonable one.

08:10.900 --> 08:13.600
So I'm going to build up an
object, which is a student.

08:13.600 --> 08:16.420
So in this lecture notes, I
think we've been consistent.

08:16.420 --> 08:17.620
I caught one or two typos.

08:17.620 --> 08:20.050
We're going to think of x
as the object that contains

08:20.050 --> 08:21.670
all of the information.

08:21.670 --> 08:24.555
And then associated
with that is one piece,

08:24.555 --> 08:25.555
which is called the key.

08:25.555 --> 08:27.957
That's where we're
going to use a letter k.

08:27.957 --> 08:29.290
And that's like your student ID.

08:29.290 --> 08:31.340
That's the thing I'm
going to use to search.

08:31.340 --> 08:33.530
So what the Insert
operation does

08:33.530 --> 08:35.289
that takes this
whole student object

08:35.289 --> 08:37.789
x, which includes your ID, your
name, your phone number, all

08:37.789 --> 08:40.358
that good stuff, and
inserts it into the set

08:40.358 --> 08:42.400
with the understanding
that when I search my set,

08:42.400 --> 08:43.995
I'm going to be
searching by key.

08:43.995 --> 08:45.370
So when I want to
find a student,

08:45.370 --> 08:47.670
I have to put in my ID number.

08:47.670 --> 08:49.660
Does that makes sense?

08:49.660 --> 08:50.187
Cool.

08:50.187 --> 08:51.020
Any other questions?

08:51.020 --> 08:53.330
That's great.

08:53.330 --> 08:54.420
Fabulous.

08:54.420 --> 08:54.920
OK.

08:54.920 --> 08:58.250
So now, let's talk about how to
actually implement this thing.

08:58.250 --> 09:01.640
And thankfully, we're already
equipped with at least a very

09:01.640 --> 09:04.905
simple way that we can implement
a set based on what you've

09:04.905 --> 09:07.280
already seen in your previous
programming classes or even

09:07.280 --> 09:10.820
just in the last two lectures,
which is one way to understand

09:10.820 --> 09:14.150
a set or to implement it
rather would be to just store

09:14.150 --> 09:21.080
a giant array of objects
that are in my set.

09:21.080 --> 09:24.350
I suppose continuing with the
theme of the last two lectures,

09:24.350 --> 09:26.300
this is not a space
in memory, but rather

09:26.300 --> 09:30.172
a metaphorical array,
a theoretical array.

09:30.172 --> 09:31.380
But it doesn't really matter.

09:31.380 --> 09:35.390
And so one way to
store my set would

09:35.390 --> 09:39.920
be to just store a bunch of
x's in no particular order.

09:39.920 --> 09:40.800
Does that make sense?

09:40.800 --> 09:42.290
So I have a big piece of memory.

09:42.290 --> 09:44.593
Every piece of
memory is associated

09:44.593 --> 09:46.010
with a different
object in my set.

09:46.010 --> 09:48.000
Obviously, this is
quite easy to build.

09:48.000 --> 09:51.140
I just make a big array and
dump everything in there.

09:51.140 --> 09:53.450
And the question is, is
this particularly efficient

09:53.450 --> 09:57.400
or a useful way to
implement a set?

09:57.400 --> 10:00.650
So for instance,
let's say that I

10:00.650 --> 10:03.118
have a set of all the
students in this classroom.

10:03.118 --> 10:04.910
There's some ridiculous
number of you guys.

10:04.910 --> 10:07.340
So actually, asymptotic
efficiency maybe

10:07.340 --> 10:08.960
actually matters a little bit.

10:08.960 --> 10:13.640
And I want to query, does this
student exist in my class?

10:13.640 --> 10:16.310
Is Erik Demaine taking 6.006?

10:16.310 --> 10:18.410
The answer is no, I think.

10:18.410 --> 10:19.160
Teaching, taking?

10:19.160 --> 10:20.700
I don't know.

10:20.700 --> 10:23.330
But in any event,
how do I implement it

10:23.330 --> 10:26.340
if my set is unordered?

10:26.340 --> 10:28.970
We'll think about
it for a second.

10:28.970 --> 10:31.232
Yeah?

10:31.232 --> 10:35.580
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

10:35.580 --> 10:37.580
JUSTIN: It's actually an
interesting suggestion,

10:37.580 --> 10:39.705
which is going to anticipate
what's happening later

10:39.705 --> 10:42.240
in this lecture, which was
to sort the set and then

10:42.240 --> 10:43.297
binary search.

10:43.297 --> 10:45.630
But let's say that actually
I only have to do this once.

10:45.630 --> 10:47.370
For some reason, I built up
a whole set of the people

10:47.370 --> 10:48.540
in this classroom.

10:48.540 --> 10:51.100
And I just want to know, is
Erik Demaine in this class?

10:51.100 --> 10:53.760
So then that algorithm
would take n log n time

10:53.760 --> 10:55.560
because I've got
to sort everybody.

10:55.560 --> 10:57.570
And then I have to do
binary search, which

10:57.570 --> 10:59.310
is maybe log n time.

10:59.310 --> 11:01.350
But I claim that, if the
only thing I care about

11:01.350 --> 11:03.480
is building up my entire
set and searching it once,

11:03.480 --> 11:04.980
there's actually a
faster algorithm.

11:04.980 --> 11:06.960
This is going to be needlessly
confusing because we're

11:06.960 --> 11:09.585
going to see that this is really
not the right way to implement

11:09.585 --> 11:11.550
it in about 38 seconds.

11:11.550 --> 11:12.305
Yes?

11:12.305 --> 11:13.180
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

11:13.180 --> 11:13.722
JUSTIN: Yeah.

11:13.722 --> 11:15.940
Just iterate from beginning
to this array and say,

11:15.940 --> 11:17.060
is this guy Erik?

11:17.060 --> 11:17.560
No.

11:17.560 --> 11:18.290
Is this guy Erik?

11:18.290 --> 11:18.790
No.

11:18.790 --> 11:19.600
Is this guy Erik?

11:19.600 --> 11:20.110
Yes.

11:20.110 --> 11:22.540
And then return it.

11:22.540 --> 11:26.260
So in the worst case, how
long will that algorithm take?

11:26.260 --> 11:29.530
Well, in the worst case
of really bad luck,

11:29.530 --> 11:33.250
your instructor is all the
way at the end of the list.

11:33.250 --> 11:35.480
So in this case, what
is that going to mean?

11:35.480 --> 11:38.230
That means that I have to
walk along the entire array

11:38.230 --> 11:39.640
before I find him.

11:39.640 --> 11:42.020
So that algorithm
takes order n time.

11:42.020 --> 11:43.480
And so your
colleague's intuition

11:43.480 --> 11:45.370
that somehow this
is quite inefficient

11:45.370 --> 11:46.590
is absolutely correct.

11:46.590 --> 11:48.340
If I know that I'm
going to have to search

11:48.340 --> 11:51.260
my array many, many times for
different people, then probably

11:51.260 --> 11:53.593
it makes sense to do a little
bit of work ahead of time,

11:53.593 --> 11:54.940
like sorting the list.

11:54.940 --> 11:58.430
And then my query is
much more efficient.

11:58.430 --> 12:00.710
But this is all just to say
that an unordered array is

12:00.710 --> 12:04.250
a perfectly reasonable way to
implement this set interface.

12:04.250 --> 12:07.010
And then searching that array
it will take linear time

12:07.010 --> 12:09.680
every single time I search.

12:09.680 --> 12:11.420
And of course, if
you go down your list

12:11.420 --> 12:13.253
of all of the different
operations you might

12:13.253 --> 12:16.885
want to do on a set, you'll see
that they all take linear time.

12:16.885 --> 12:18.510
So for instance, how
do I build myself?

12:18.510 --> 12:22.400
Well, I have to reserve
n slots in memory.

12:22.400 --> 12:25.250
And at least
according to our model

12:25.250 --> 12:28.480
of computation in this class,
that takes order n time.

12:28.480 --> 12:31.070
Then I'm going to copy
everything into the set.

12:31.070 --> 12:33.165
Similarly, if I want
to insert or delete,

12:33.165 --> 12:34.040
what do I have to do?

12:34.040 --> 12:37.003
Well, I have to reserve
memory, stick something

12:37.003 --> 12:37.670
inside of there.

12:37.670 --> 12:40.940
In the worst case, we saw
this amortize argument before,

12:40.940 --> 12:43.460
if your set is allowed
to grow dynamically.

12:43.460 --> 12:46.350
And finally, if I wanted
to find the minimum student

12:46.350 --> 12:49.960
ID in my classroom,
the only algorithm

12:49.960 --> 12:53.320
I can have if my list
of students isn't sorted

12:53.320 --> 12:53.950
is to what?

12:53.950 --> 12:56.313
Just iterate over every
single student in the class.

12:56.313 --> 12:57.730
And if the guy
that I'm looking at

12:57.730 --> 13:01.903
has a smaller ID than the
one that I found, replace it.

13:01.903 --> 13:03.320
Does that make
sense to everybody?

13:03.320 --> 13:06.070
So basically, everything you can
do in a set you can implement--

13:06.070 --> 13:07.778
and I think all of
you guys are more than

13:07.778 --> 13:10.300
qualified to implement--
as an unordered array.

13:10.300 --> 13:11.640
It's just going to be slow.

13:11.640 --> 13:13.054
Yes?

13:13.054 --> 13:17.235
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

13:17.235 --> 13:18.360
JUSTIN: Yeah, that's right.

13:18.360 --> 13:21.675
So actually, I don't
know in this class.

13:21.675 --> 13:23.550
I guess, the interface
and the way that we've

13:23.550 --> 13:24.758
described it here is dynamic.

13:24.758 --> 13:26.970
We can just keep
adding stuff to it.

13:26.970 --> 13:29.040
In that case, remember
this amortized argument

13:29.040 --> 13:31.070
from Erik's lecture
says that on average

13:31.070 --> 13:34.080
that it will take order n time.

13:34.080 --> 13:35.840
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

13:35.840 --> 13:37.177
JUSTIN: What was that?

13:37.177 --> 13:38.300
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

13:38.300 --> 13:39.300
JUSTIN: Oh, that's true.

13:39.300 --> 13:40.890
That's an even better-- sorry.

13:40.890 --> 13:43.560
Even if it weren't dynamic.

13:43.560 --> 13:46.523
If I wanted to replace
an existing key--

13:46.523 --> 13:48.690
like, for some reason, two
students had the same ID.

13:48.690 --> 13:50.010
This is a terrible analogy.

13:50.010 --> 13:51.310
I'm sorry.

13:51.310 --> 13:53.610
But in any event, if
I wanted to replace

13:53.610 --> 13:56.153
an object with a new one,
well, what would I have to do?

13:56.153 --> 13:57.570
I'd have to search
for that object

13:57.570 --> 13:59.130
first, and then replace it.

13:59.130 --> 14:01.830
And that search is going to take
order n time from our argument

14:01.830 --> 14:02.370
before.

14:02.370 --> 14:04.300
Thank you.

14:04.300 --> 14:04.800
OK.

14:04.800 --> 14:07.450
So in some sense, we're done.

14:07.450 --> 14:09.150
We've now implemented
the interface.

14:09.150 --> 14:10.788
Life is good.

14:10.788 --> 14:12.330
And of course, this
is the difference

14:12.330 --> 14:16.290
between existence and actually
caring about the details

14:16.290 --> 14:17.170
inside of this thing.

14:17.170 --> 14:19.578
We've shown that one
can implement a set.

14:19.578 --> 14:21.120
But it's not a
terribly efficient way

14:21.120 --> 14:24.210
to do it by just storing a big,
hot mess, disorganized list

14:24.210 --> 14:26.850
of numbers without any order.

14:26.850 --> 14:29.242
So instead of
that, conveniently,

14:29.242 --> 14:30.700
our colleague in
the front row here

14:30.700 --> 14:33.700
has already suggested a
different data structure,

14:33.700 --> 14:37.530
which is to store our set not
as just a disorganized array

14:37.530 --> 14:41.670
in any arbitrary order, but
rather to keep the items

14:41.670 --> 14:44.690
in our set organized by key.

14:44.690 --> 14:47.020
So in other words, if I
have this array of all

14:47.020 --> 14:48.700
of the students
in our classroom,

14:48.700 --> 14:50.180
the very first
element in my array

14:50.180 --> 14:52.660
is going to be the student
with the smallest ID number,

14:52.660 --> 14:54.677
the second is the second
smallest number, all

14:54.677 --> 14:56.260
the way to the end
of the array, which

14:56.260 --> 14:59.080
is the student with
the biggest ID number.

14:59.080 --> 15:02.170
Now, does that mean I
want to do arithmetic

15:02.170 --> 15:03.130
on student ID numbers?

15:03.130 --> 15:04.060
Absolutely not.

15:04.060 --> 15:06.910
But it's just a way to
impose order on that list

15:06.910 --> 15:09.810
so that I can search
it very quickly later.

15:09.810 --> 15:10.310
OK.

15:10.310 --> 15:13.640
So if I want to fill
in the set interface

15:13.640 --> 15:17.790
and I have somehow a
sorted array of students--

15:17.790 --> 15:20.960
so again, they're organized
by student ID number--

15:20.960 --> 15:24.440
then my runtime starts to get
a little more interesting.

15:24.440 --> 15:24.940
Yeah.

15:24.940 --> 15:27.272
So now, insertion,
deletion they'd still

15:27.272 --> 15:28.480
take the same amount of time.

15:28.480 --> 15:29.855
But let's say that
I want to find

15:29.855 --> 15:34.290
the student with the minimum ID
number, this find min function.

15:34.290 --> 15:36.900
Well, how could I do
it in a sorted array?

15:36.900 --> 15:39.490
Keyword is sorted here.

15:39.490 --> 15:41.280
Where's the min
element of an array?

15:41.280 --> 15:42.500
Yes?

15:42.500 --> 15:44.458
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

15:44.458 --> 15:45.000
JUSTIN: Yeah.

15:45.000 --> 15:47.333
In fact, I can give a moderately
faster algorithm, which

15:47.333 --> 15:49.170
is just look at the first one.

15:49.170 --> 15:52.740
If I want the minimum element
of an array and the array

15:52.740 --> 15:55.760
is in sorted order, I know
that's the first thing.

15:55.760 --> 15:58.260
So that's order 1 time to
answer that kind of a question.

15:58.260 --> 16:00.840
And similarly, if I want the
thing with the biggest ID

16:00.840 --> 16:03.240
number, I look all
the way at the end.

16:03.240 --> 16:06.300
Now, in 6.006--

16:06.300 --> 16:10.320
MIT student class numbers
are super confusing to me.

16:10.320 --> 16:12.930
In 6.0001, 6.042,
you guys already

16:12.930 --> 16:14.910
I think learned
about binary search

16:14.910 --> 16:18.318
and even may have
implemented it.

16:18.318 --> 16:19.110
So what do we know?

16:19.110 --> 16:21.330
If my array is sorted,
how long does it

16:21.330 --> 16:24.950
take for me to search
for any given element?

16:24.950 --> 16:25.600
Yes?

16:25.600 --> 16:26.300
AUDIENCE: Log n time.

16:26.300 --> 16:27.092
JUSTIN: Log n time.

16:27.092 --> 16:29.690
That's absolutely right because
I can cut my array in half.

16:29.690 --> 16:31.970
If my key is bigger
or smaller, then I

16:31.970 --> 16:33.960
look on the left or the right.

16:33.960 --> 16:38.360
And so this is a much
more efficient means

16:38.360 --> 16:40.850
of searching a set.

16:40.850 --> 16:44.870
So in particular, 6.006
this year has 400 students.

16:44.870 --> 16:46.430
Maybe next year, it has 4,000.

16:46.430 --> 16:49.850
And eventually, it's
going to have billions.

16:49.850 --> 16:51.360
Then what's going to happen?

16:51.360 --> 16:53.477
Well, if I use my
unordered array

16:53.477 --> 16:55.310
and I have a billion
students in this class,

16:55.310 --> 16:56.990
what's going to happen?

16:56.990 --> 16:59.960
Well, then it's going to take me
roughly a billion computations

16:59.960 --> 17:02.570
to find any one
student in this course,

17:02.570 --> 17:06.619
whereas log of a billion
is a heck of a lot faster.

17:06.619 --> 17:10.250
On the other hand, I've
swept under the rug

17:10.250 --> 17:12.920
here, which is how
do I actually get

17:12.920 --> 17:14.128
a sorted array to begin with.

17:14.128 --> 17:16.045
And what we're going to
see in today's lecture

17:16.045 --> 17:18.770
is that that takes more
time than building if I just

17:18.770 --> 17:19.823
have a disorganized list.

17:19.823 --> 17:21.990
Building a disorganized
list is an easy thing to do.

17:21.990 --> 17:25.575
You probably all do it at home
when you're cleaning house.

17:25.575 --> 17:27.200
But actually, sorting
a list of numbers

17:27.200 --> 17:29.072
requires a little bit more work.

17:29.072 --> 17:31.280
And so this is a great
example where there's at least

17:31.280 --> 17:33.710
a tiny amount of tradeoff.

17:33.710 --> 17:36.218
Now, building my sorted
array to represent my set

17:36.218 --> 17:38.010
is going to take a
little more computation.

17:38.010 --> 17:39.980
We're going to see
it's n log n time.

17:39.980 --> 17:42.740
But then once I've
done that step 0,

17:42.740 --> 17:44.828
now a lot of these
other operations

17:44.828 --> 17:46.370
that I typically
care about in a set,

17:46.370 --> 17:48.020
like searching it
for a given key,

17:48.020 --> 17:53.740
are going to go a lot
faster using binary search.

17:53.740 --> 17:56.690
So this is our basic
motivator here.

17:56.690 --> 17:59.530
And so now, we've seen the
setup interface and two

17:59.530 --> 18:01.000
potential data structures.

18:01.000 --> 18:02.620
And our goal for
the day is going

18:02.620 --> 18:06.040
to be to fill in the
details of that second one.

18:06.040 --> 18:08.440
And since you all have
already seen binary search,

18:08.440 --> 18:10.420
you've probably also
already seen sorting.

18:10.420 --> 18:12.520
But in any event,
today, we're going

18:12.520 --> 18:15.760
to focus mostly on
the lower left square

18:15.760 --> 18:19.990
here, on just how can I take
a disorganized list of objects

18:19.990 --> 18:23.650
and put it into sorted order so
that I can search for it later.

18:23.650 --> 18:27.820
So in other words, our big
problem for lecture today

18:27.820 --> 18:30.865
is the second thing
here, this sorting.

18:30.865 --> 18:32.740
Incidentally, in the
next couple of lectures,

18:32.740 --> 18:34.810
we're going to see other
data sets-- or data

18:34.810 --> 18:35.950
structures, rather.

18:35.950 --> 18:37.180
Sorry, data sets.

18:37.180 --> 18:39.610
I used to teach
machine learning class.

18:39.610 --> 18:43.998
And we'll see that they have
different efficiency operations

18:43.998 --> 18:45.290
that we can fill in this table.

18:45.290 --> 18:46.207
So we're not done yet.

18:46.207 --> 18:48.670
But this is one step forward.

18:48.670 --> 18:49.480
OK.

18:49.480 --> 18:52.330
So hopefully, I have
ad nauseum justified

18:52.330 --> 18:54.327
why one might want
to sort things.

18:54.327 --> 18:56.410
And indeed, there are a
couple of vocabulary words

18:56.410 --> 18:57.560
that are worth noting.

18:57.560 --> 19:00.760
So one, so remember that
your sorting algorithm

19:00.760 --> 19:03.940
is pretty straightforward in
terms of how you specify it.

19:03.940 --> 19:15.550
So in sorting, your input
is an array of n numbers.

19:15.550 --> 19:17.170
I suppose actually
really that we

19:17.170 --> 19:18.462
should think of them like keys.

19:18.462 --> 19:21.100
It's not going to
matter a whole lot.

19:21.100 --> 19:21.790
And our output--

19:24.662 --> 19:26.120
I'm always very
concerned that if I

19:26.120 --> 19:30.650
write on the board on the
back, I have to cover it up--

19:30.650 --> 19:41.520
is going to be sorted array.

19:41.520 --> 19:44.130
And we'll call this
guy B. We'll call

19:44.130 --> 19:49.370
this one A. This classroom is
not optimized for short people.

19:51.890 --> 19:54.140
So there's a lot of variations
on the basics sorting

19:54.140 --> 19:56.613
problem and the different
algorithms that are out there.

19:56.613 --> 19:59.030
Two vocabulary words are going
to highlight really quick--

19:59.030 --> 20:01.100
one is if your sort
is destructive,

20:01.100 --> 20:04.820
what that means is that rather
than reserving some new memory

20:04.820 --> 20:08.930
for my sorted array B and then
putting a sorted version of A

20:08.930 --> 20:13.250
into B, a destructive algorithm
is one that just overwrites A

20:13.250 --> 20:18.300
with a sorted version of A.
Certainly the C++ interface

20:18.300 --> 20:19.080
does this.

20:19.080 --> 20:20.970
I assume the Python
one does, too.

20:20.970 --> 20:25.350
I always forget this detail.

20:25.350 --> 20:27.990
In addition to
destructive sorts,

20:27.990 --> 20:31.880
some sorts are in place,
meaning that not only are

20:31.880 --> 20:34.490
they destructive, but they
also don't use extra memory

20:34.490 --> 20:35.825
in the process of sorting.

20:35.825 --> 20:37.700
Really, you could imagine
a sorting algorithm

20:37.700 --> 20:40.850
that has to reserve a bunch of
scratch space to do its work,

20:40.850 --> 20:44.060
and then put it back into A.

20:44.060 --> 20:47.240
For instance, the world's
dumbest destructive sort

20:47.240 --> 20:49.070
might be to call
your non-destructive

20:49.070 --> 20:52.820
and then copy it back
into A. But that would

20:52.820 --> 20:55.220
require order n space to do.

20:55.220 --> 20:56.960
So if my algorithm
additionally has

20:56.960 --> 20:59.510
the property that it doesn't
reserve any extra space,

20:59.510 --> 21:03.740
at least up to a constant,
then we call that in place.

21:03.740 --> 21:04.240
OK.

21:04.240 --> 21:05.800
So those are our basic
vocabulary words.

21:05.800 --> 21:07.600
And they're ways to
understand the differences

21:07.600 --> 21:09.142
between different
sorting algorithms.

21:09.142 --> 21:09.662
Yes?

21:09.662 --> 21:12.760
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

21:12.760 --> 21:14.890
JUSTIN: Why do they end
up using extra O(1) space?

21:14.890 --> 21:15.550
Oh yeah, sure.

21:15.550 --> 21:18.040
Any time I just make
a temporary variable

21:18.040 --> 21:20.110
like a loop counter,
that's going

21:20.110 --> 21:21.547
to count toward that order 1.

21:21.547 --> 21:24.130
But the important thing is that
the number of variables I need

21:24.130 --> 21:28.030
doesn't scale in the
length of the list.

21:28.030 --> 21:28.630
OK.

21:28.630 --> 21:30.400
So I present to you
the beginning and end

21:30.400 --> 21:33.610
of our sorting lecture, which
is the world's simplest sorting

21:33.610 --> 21:34.750
algorithm.

21:34.750 --> 21:36.648
I call it permutation sort.

21:36.648 --> 21:38.440
I think it's very easy
to prove correctness

21:38.440 --> 21:40.630
for this particular technique.

21:40.630 --> 21:43.250
So in permutation
sort, what can I do?

21:43.250 --> 21:47.920
Well, I know that if I have an
input that's a list of numbers,

21:47.920 --> 21:51.250
there exists a permutation
of that list of numbers that

21:51.250 --> 21:54.720
is sorted by definition
because a sort is

21:54.720 --> 21:57.300
a permutation of
your original list.

21:57.300 --> 21:59.600
So what's a very simple
sorting algorithm?

21:59.600 --> 22:04.840
Well, list every
possible permutation,

22:04.840 --> 22:08.370
and then just double check
which one's in the right order.

22:08.370 --> 22:11.948
So there's two key pieces to
this particular technique,

22:11.948 --> 22:12.990
if we want to analyze it.

22:12.990 --> 22:15.240
I don't see a reason
to belabor it too much.

22:15.240 --> 22:22.560
But one is that we have to
enumerate the permutations.

22:25.090 --> 22:27.010
Now, if I have a
list of n numbers,

22:27.010 --> 22:30.160
how many different permutations
of n numbers are there?

22:30.160 --> 22:30.880
Yes?

22:30.880 --> 22:31.810
AUDIENCE: n factorial.

22:31.810 --> 22:33.970
JUSTIN: n factorial.

22:33.970 --> 22:38.570
So just by virtue of calling
this permutation's function,

22:38.570 --> 22:42.200
I know that I incur at
least n factorial time.

22:42.200 --> 22:43.140
It might be worse.

22:43.140 --> 22:45.530
It might be that like actually
listing permutations takes

22:45.530 --> 22:47.738
a lot of time for some
reason, like every permutation

22:47.738 --> 22:49.340
itself takes order n time.

22:49.340 --> 22:52.310
But at the very least,
each one of these things

22:52.310 --> 22:54.500
looks like n factorial.

22:54.500 --> 22:57.210
I warned you my
handwriting is terrible.

22:57.210 --> 23:00.920
So that's what this omega thing
is doing, if I recall properly.

23:00.920 --> 23:03.170
And then secondarily,
well, we've

23:03.170 --> 23:08.600
got to check if that particular
permutation is sorted.

23:16.730 --> 23:19.370
How are we going to do that?

23:19.370 --> 23:21.950
There's a very easy way to
check if a list is sorted.

23:21.950 --> 23:29.210
I'm going to do maybe for
i equals 1 to n minus 1.

23:29.210 --> 23:30.350
Notice not a Python coder.

23:30.350 --> 23:32.230
It's going to look different.

23:32.230 --> 23:44.000
Then check, is Bi less
than or equal to Bi plus 1?

23:44.000 --> 23:48.050
And so if this relationship
is true for every single i--

23:48.050 --> 23:49.660
that's supposed to
be a question mark.

23:49.660 --> 23:52.160
This was less than or equal to
with a question mark over it.

23:52.160 --> 23:53.755
There's my special notation.

23:53.755 --> 23:55.880
So if I get all the way to
the end of this for loop

23:55.880 --> 23:58.550
and this is true everywhere,
then my list is sorted

23:58.550 --> 24:01.770
and life is good.

24:01.770 --> 24:03.390
So how long does
this algorithm take?

24:03.390 --> 24:04.950
Well, it's staring
you right in the face

24:04.950 --> 24:06.450
because you have
an algorithm, which

24:06.450 --> 24:09.030
is looping from 1 to n minus 1.

24:09.030 --> 24:13.920
So this step incurs order n
time because theta of n time

24:13.920 --> 24:16.870
because it's got to go all the
way to the end of the list.

24:16.870 --> 24:20.040
So when I put these things
together, permutation sort--

24:20.040 --> 24:23.430
well, remember that this
check if sorted happens

24:23.430 --> 24:25.830
for every single permutation.

24:25.830 --> 24:27.510
So at the end of the
day, our algorithm

24:27.510 --> 24:34.535
takes at least n
factorial times n time.

24:34.535 --> 24:35.910
It's a great
example of something

24:35.910 --> 24:39.160
that's even worse than
n factorial, which

24:39.160 --> 24:43.490
somehow in my head is like
the worst possible algorithm.

24:43.490 --> 24:46.470
So do you think that Python
implements permutation sort?

24:46.470 --> 24:48.390
I certainly hope not.

24:48.390 --> 24:49.640
Yes?

24:49.640 --> 24:51.477
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

24:51.477 --> 24:52.060
JUSTIN: Right.

24:52.060 --> 24:54.370
So the question was, why
is it omega and not big O?

24:54.370 --> 24:56.750
Which is a fabulous
question in this course.

24:56.750 --> 24:58.300
So here's the basic issue.

24:58.300 --> 25:00.040
I haven't given you
an algorithm for how

25:00.040 --> 25:02.123
to compute the set
of permutations

25:02.123 --> 25:03.040
for a list of numbers.

25:03.040 --> 25:06.310
I just called some magic
function that I made up.

25:06.310 --> 25:09.490
But I know that that algorithm
takes at least n factorial time

25:09.490 --> 25:10.210
in some sense.

25:10.210 --> 25:12.190
Or if nothing else, the
list of permutations

25:12.190 --> 25:14.200
is n factorial
big because that's

25:14.200 --> 25:16.290
all the stuff has to compute.

25:16.290 --> 25:18.290
So I haven't told you how
to solve this problem.

25:18.290 --> 25:20.750
But I'm convinced that it's
at least this amount of time.

25:20.750 --> 25:23.350
So remember that omega
means lower bound.

25:23.350 --> 25:26.380
So when I put it all
together, in some sense--

25:26.380 --> 25:28.075
OK, this isn't
satisfying in the sense

25:28.075 --> 25:30.700
that I didn't give you precisely
the runtime of this algorithm.

25:30.700 --> 25:33.650
But hopefully, I've convinced
you that it's super useless.

25:33.650 --> 25:35.300
Yeah, OK.

25:35.300 --> 25:38.660
Any other questions about that?

25:38.660 --> 25:39.380
But great.

25:39.380 --> 25:42.507
So if we go back to our
table for the set interface,

25:42.507 --> 25:44.090
well, in some sense,
if we implemented

25:44.090 --> 25:47.720
it using this goofy algorithm,
then the lower left entry

25:47.720 --> 25:49.610
in our table would
be n factorial times

25:49.610 --> 25:51.410
n, which wouldn't be so hot.

25:51.410 --> 25:54.860
But notice that actually all
the rest of our operations

25:54.860 --> 25:55.920
are now quite efficient.

25:55.920 --> 25:57.020
I can use binary search.

25:57.020 --> 25:59.900
I just obtained the
algorithm that--

25:59.900 --> 26:03.860
rather, I obtained the sorted
array in a funny fashion.

26:03.860 --> 26:04.360
OK.

26:04.360 --> 26:06.880
So let's fill in some more
interesting algorithms.

26:06.880 --> 26:08.620
As usual, I'm talking too much.

26:08.620 --> 26:09.940
And I'm nervous about the time.

26:09.940 --> 26:13.120
But we can skip one
of them if we need to.

26:13.120 --> 26:17.490
So how many of us have
seen selection sort before?

26:17.490 --> 26:18.370
I see your hand.

26:18.370 --> 26:20.530
But we're going to
defer for a little bit.

26:20.530 --> 26:21.950
I'm sorry?

26:21.950 --> 26:23.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

26:23.340 --> 26:24.340
JUSTIN: That's fabulous.

26:24.340 --> 26:26.048
Why don't we defer to
the end of lecture?

26:26.048 --> 26:27.320
And we'll do it then.

26:27.320 --> 26:27.820
OK.

26:27.820 --> 26:31.120
So the first algorithm that
we'll talk about for sorting,

26:31.120 --> 26:33.250
which is somewhat
sensible, is something

26:33.250 --> 26:34.840
called selection sort.

26:34.840 --> 26:37.310
Selection sort is exactly
what it sounds like.

26:37.310 --> 26:40.840
So let's say that we have a
list of-- whoops, my laptop

26:40.840 --> 26:42.250
and the screen are not agreeing.

26:42.250 --> 26:42.650
OK.

26:42.650 --> 26:44.150
Let's say I have a
list of numbers--

26:44.150 --> 26:46.510
8, 2, 4, 9, 3.

26:46.510 --> 26:48.333
There's a message
that Jason I think

26:48.333 --> 26:49.750
is sending me in
the course notes.

26:49.750 --> 26:52.430
But I haven't figured it out.

26:52.430 --> 26:56.658
But in any event, I want to
sort this list of numbers.

26:56.658 --> 26:58.450
Here's a simple algorithm
for how to do it,

26:58.450 --> 27:02.020
which is I can find the biggest
number in this whole list

27:02.020 --> 27:04.110
and stick it at the end.

27:04.110 --> 27:05.860
So in this case, what's
the biggest number

27:05.860 --> 27:07.840
in this list everybody?

27:07.840 --> 27:08.480
9.

27:08.480 --> 27:08.980
Good.

27:08.980 --> 27:10.960
See, this is why you go to MIT.

27:10.960 --> 27:13.120
So I'm going to take that 9.

27:13.120 --> 27:13.720
I find it.

27:13.720 --> 27:17.760
And then swap it out with
the 3, which is at the end.

27:17.760 --> 27:19.762
And now, what's my
inductive hypothesis?

27:19.762 --> 27:21.470
Well, in some sense,
it's that everything

27:21.470 --> 27:23.845
to the right of this little
red line that I've drawn here

27:23.845 --> 27:27.270
is in sorted order, in this case
because there's only one thing.

27:27.270 --> 27:28.520
So now, what am I going to do?

27:28.520 --> 27:30.437
I'm going to look to the
left of the red line,

27:30.437 --> 27:32.200
find the next biggest thing.

27:32.200 --> 27:35.050
What's that?

27:35.050 --> 27:35.800
Come on.

27:35.800 --> 27:36.917
AUDIENCE: 8.

27:36.917 --> 27:37.750
JUSTIN: There we go.

27:37.750 --> 27:38.690
Yeah, wake up.

27:38.690 --> 27:39.190
OK.

27:39.190 --> 27:41.300
So right, the next
biggest one is the 8.

27:41.300 --> 27:44.560
So we're going to swap it
with the 3, put it at the end,

27:44.560 --> 27:45.160
and so on.

27:45.160 --> 27:47.268
I think you guys could
all finish this off.

27:47.268 --> 27:49.810
I suppose there should be one
last line here where everything

27:49.810 --> 27:51.190
is green and we're happy.

27:51.190 --> 27:54.610
But in some sense, we're pretty
sure that an array of one item

27:54.610 --> 27:56.680
is in sorted order.

27:56.680 --> 27:59.490
And so essentially, from a high
level, what does selection sort

27:59.490 --> 28:00.370
do?

28:00.370 --> 28:03.520
Well, it just kept choosing the
element which was the biggest

28:03.520 --> 28:07.060
and swapping it into the
back and then iterating.

28:07.060 --> 28:10.270
Now, in 6.006, we're going to
write selection sort in a way

28:10.270 --> 28:12.640
that you might not
be familiar with.

28:12.640 --> 28:15.280
In some sense, this is not
so hard to implement with two

28:15.280 --> 28:15.790
for loops.

28:15.790 --> 28:17.582
I think you guys could
all do this at home.

28:17.582 --> 28:18.850
In fact, you may have already.

28:18.850 --> 28:20.600
But in this class,
because we're concerned

28:20.600 --> 28:22.990
with proving correctness,
proving efficiency,

28:22.990 --> 28:25.000
all that good stuff,
we're going to write it

28:25.000 --> 28:29.120
in kind of a funny way,
which is recursive.

28:29.120 --> 28:33.770
Now, I can't emphasize strongly
enough how little you guys

28:33.770 --> 28:35.130
should implement this at home.

28:35.130 --> 28:37.730
This is mostly a theoretical
version of selection sort

28:37.730 --> 28:38.990
rather than one that
you would actually

28:38.990 --> 28:40.910
want to write in code
because there's obviously

28:40.910 --> 28:41.720
a much better way to do it.

28:41.720 --> 28:43.762
And you'll see that in
your recitation this week,

28:43.762 --> 28:45.150
I believe.

28:45.150 --> 28:47.060
But in terms of
analysis, there's

28:47.060 --> 28:49.370
a nice, easy way
to write it down.

28:49.370 --> 28:53.150
So we're going to take the
selection sort algorithm.

28:53.150 --> 28:55.770
And we're going to divide
it into two chunks.

28:55.770 --> 29:00.350
One of them is find me the
biggest thing in the first k

29:00.350 --> 29:01.402
elements of my array.

29:01.402 --> 29:03.110
I shouldn't use k
because that means key.

29:03.110 --> 29:05.690
The first i elements
of my array.

29:05.690 --> 29:07.628
And the next one is
to swap it into place

29:07.628 --> 29:09.170
and then sort
everything to the left.

29:09.170 --> 29:10.348
That's the two pieces here.

29:10.348 --> 29:11.390
So let's write that down.

29:14.450 --> 29:16.670
So what did I do?

29:16.670 --> 29:20.180
Well, in some sense,
in step 1 here, I

29:20.180 --> 29:31.240
found the biggest with index
less than or equal to i.

29:31.240 --> 29:35.860
So I started at the end of the
list, and then moved backward.

29:35.860 --> 29:41.292
And then step 2 was to
swap that into place.

29:41.292 --> 29:43.000
Notice when I say
swap-- so for instance,

29:43.000 --> 29:44.803
when I put the 8
there, well, I had

29:44.803 --> 29:45.970
to do something with that 3.

29:45.970 --> 29:48.740
So I just put it where
the 8 used to be.

29:48.740 --> 29:53.540
And then finally,
well, am I done?

29:53.540 --> 29:56.120
No, I just put the biggest
thing at the end of my array.

29:56.120 --> 30:00.350
So now, I have to sort
from index 1 to i minus 1

30:00.350 --> 30:03.260
because now I know that the
last guy is in sorted order.

30:03.260 --> 30:03.770
I see you.

30:03.770 --> 30:05.690
I'll turn it over to
you in just a sec.

30:11.170 --> 30:12.436
Yes?

30:12.436 --> 30:13.750
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

30:13.750 --> 30:15.688
JUSTIN: You can't
read the handwriting?

30:15.688 --> 30:17.020
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

30:17.020 --> 30:19.750
JUSTIN: This is index
less than or equal to i.

30:19.750 --> 30:21.788
Great question.

30:21.788 --> 30:22.330
I warned you.

30:22.330 --> 30:25.270
It's going to be a problem.

30:25.270 --> 30:29.600
So let's do step 1 first.

30:29.600 --> 30:31.300
So I'm going to put
code on the board.

30:31.300 --> 30:34.860
And then we're going
to fill in the details.

30:34.860 --> 30:36.900
Erik is posting on Facebook.

30:36.900 --> 30:39.780
I'm going to turn that
feature off on my watch later.

30:39.780 --> 30:45.960
So right, let's implement
this helper function here.

30:45.960 --> 30:48.253
This is something we're
going to call prefix max.

30:48.253 --> 30:49.920
And this is going to
find me the biggest

30:49.920 --> 30:54.970
element of array between
index 0 and index i inclusive,

30:54.970 --> 30:55.470
I believe.

30:55.470 --> 30:56.272
Yeah?

30:56.272 --> 30:57.690
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

30:57.690 --> 30:59.730
JUSTIN: Well, here's an
interesting observation,

30:59.730 --> 31:09.200
really a deep one, which is
that the biggest element from 0

31:09.200 --> 31:10.970
to i--

31:10.970 --> 31:13.670
that's an i, sorry.

31:13.670 --> 31:15.950
There's two cases.

31:15.950 --> 31:27.370
Either it's at index i,
meaning I have the first 10

31:27.370 --> 31:28.720
elements of my right--

31:28.720 --> 31:34.760
either it is element number
10 or what's the other case?

31:34.760 --> 31:36.220
It ain't, Yeah?

31:36.220 --> 31:47.020
In other words, it
has index less than i.

31:47.020 --> 31:48.440
This is a tautology, rate?

31:48.440 --> 31:50.840
Either the biggest thing is
at this index or it's not.

31:50.840 --> 31:52.600
In which case, it has
to be to the left.

31:52.600 --> 31:54.690
Does that makes sense?

31:54.690 --> 31:57.200
So this gives us a
really simple algorithm

31:57.200 --> 32:00.950
for finding the biggest element
in the array between index 0

32:00.950 --> 32:03.647
and index i, which is what I've
shown you on the screen here.

32:03.647 --> 32:04.730
I'd write it on the board.

32:04.730 --> 32:08.070
But I am a slow writer
and already low on time.

32:08.070 --> 32:10.490
And so essentially,
what did I implement?

32:10.490 --> 32:15.770
Well, I found the biggest
element between index 0

32:15.770 --> 32:18.170
and index i minus 1.

32:18.170 --> 32:20.345
So let's say that
I have an array--

32:22.873 --> 32:24.290
I forget the
sequence of numbers--

32:24.290 --> 32:27.560
8, 3, 5, 7, 9.

32:27.560 --> 32:29.270
That'll do it.

32:29.270 --> 32:33.680
And so like I give a
pointer here, which is i.

32:33.680 --> 32:35.690
And the very first
thing that I do

32:35.690 --> 32:38.150
is I compute the biggest
number all the way to the left

32:38.150 --> 32:39.320
of this stuff.

32:39.320 --> 32:40.430
In this case, that is?

32:40.430 --> 32:41.370
AUDIENCE: 8.

32:41.370 --> 32:41.970
JUSTIN: 8.

32:41.970 --> 32:44.340
There we go.

32:44.340 --> 32:48.210
Now, I look at the very last
element of my array, which is--

32:48.210 --> 32:48.820
9.

32:48.820 --> 32:51.390
You're killing me today, guys.

32:51.390 --> 32:52.600
And then what do I return?

32:52.600 --> 32:57.370
Well, I want the biggest
one between 0 and index i.

32:57.370 --> 32:59.490
So in this case, I return the 9.

32:59.490 --> 33:02.570
Does that make sense?

33:02.570 --> 33:04.850
So I know Jerry Cain
at Stanford likes

33:04.850 --> 33:09.230
to talk about the recursive
leap of faith that happens.

33:09.230 --> 33:11.810
Another term for
this is induction.

33:11.810 --> 33:14.613
So we want to prove that
our algorithm works.

33:14.613 --> 33:15.780
Well, what do we have to do?

33:15.780 --> 33:17.930
We have to show that when
I call this function,

33:17.930 --> 33:22.010
it gives me the max of
my array between index 0

33:22.010 --> 33:24.747
and index i for all i.

33:24.747 --> 33:27.330
So let's maybe do this inductive
proof a little bit carefully.

33:27.330 --> 33:29.790
And then the rest, we'll
be sloppy about it.

33:29.790 --> 33:36.200
So the base case is i equals 0.

33:36.200 --> 33:38.595
Well, in this case, there's
only one element in my array.

33:38.595 --> 33:40.220
So it's pretty clear
that it's the max.

33:46.110 --> 33:51.150
And now, we have to do
our inductive step, which

33:51.150 --> 33:54.570
means that if I call
prefix max with i minus 1,

33:54.570 --> 33:58.230
I really do get the max
of my array between 0

33:58.230 --> 34:00.330
and index i minus 1.

34:00.330 --> 34:06.190
And then really, I can just
look at my very deep statement,

34:06.190 --> 34:09.239
which is that either my object
is at the end of the array

34:09.239 --> 34:09.830
or it's not.

34:12.443 --> 34:13.860
And this is precisely
what we need

34:13.860 --> 34:16.020
to justify the inductive step.

34:16.020 --> 34:17.489
Essentially, there
are two cases.

34:17.489 --> 34:20.520
Either the biggest element of
my arrays the last one or it's

34:20.520 --> 34:21.909
not.

34:21.909 --> 34:24.100
We already, by our
inductive hypothesis,

34:24.100 --> 34:25.719
have argued that
our code can find

34:25.719 --> 34:31.380
the biggest element between
index 0 and index i minus 1.

34:31.380 --> 34:33.960
So as long as we take the max
of that and the very last guy,

34:33.960 --> 34:35.620
we're in good shape.

34:35.620 --> 34:40.449
So this is our very informal
proof of correctness.

34:40.449 --> 34:40.949
OK.

34:40.949 --> 34:44.070
So now, we have to justify
runtime for this algorithm.

34:44.070 --> 34:46.199
And that's actually not
100% obvious from the way

34:46.199 --> 34:47.074
I've written it here.

34:47.074 --> 34:49.330
There's no for loop.

34:49.330 --> 34:50.170
But what do I do?

34:50.170 --> 34:52.679
Well, in some sense,
if my run time

34:52.679 --> 34:56.350
is a function s,
well, for one thing,

34:56.350 --> 34:59.500
if my array has
one element in it,

34:59.500 --> 35:02.680
well, my run time might
be 7, might be 23.

35:02.680 --> 35:04.970
But at the end of the day,
it only does one thing.

35:04.970 --> 35:06.970
It just returns i.

35:06.970 --> 35:11.270
So in other words,
it's theta of 1.

35:11.270 --> 35:12.950
This isn't terribly insightful.

35:12.950 --> 35:14.220
But what else do we know?

35:14.220 --> 35:17.990
Well, when I call my function,
I call it recursively on

35:17.990 --> 35:19.430
one smaller index.

35:19.430 --> 35:21.480
And then I do a
constant amount of work.

35:21.480 --> 35:26.870
So I know that s of n is
equal to s of n minus 1

35:26.870 --> 35:29.180
plus theta of 1.

35:29.180 --> 35:32.046
I do a little bit of extra
computation on top of that.

35:32.046 --> 35:36.950
Can anybody guess what this
total runtime is going to be?

35:36.950 --> 35:37.685
Yes?

35:37.685 --> 35:38.560
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

35:38.560 --> 35:40.090
JUSTIN: Yeah, order n.

35:40.090 --> 35:42.600
So let's say that we hypothesize
that this takes n time.

35:42.600 --> 35:46.240
You can see that because at
step n we call n minus 1,

35:46.240 --> 35:51.226
we call it minus 2, and so
on, all the way down to 1.

35:51.226 --> 35:53.458
If we want to prove this,
one of the ways that we--

35:53.458 --> 35:55.750
I think, in theory, you guys
have learned in the past--

35:55.750 --> 35:58.270
and you're going to
cover it in recitation--

35:58.270 --> 36:00.590
is a technique
called substitution.

36:00.590 --> 36:03.170
What we do is we're going to
look at this relationship.

36:03.170 --> 36:06.700
And we're going to hypothesize
that we think s of n

36:06.700 --> 36:11.590
maybe look something like
cn for some constant c that

36:11.590 --> 36:13.417
doesn't depend on n.

36:13.417 --> 36:15.000
Then all we have to
do is double check

36:15.000 --> 36:16.710
that that relationship
is consistent

36:16.710 --> 36:19.440
with our inductive
hypothesis, or rather

36:19.440 --> 36:20.830
just as a recursive function.

36:20.830 --> 36:23.290
And if it is, then
we're in good shape.

36:23.290 --> 36:26.260
So in this case,
well, what do I know?

36:26.260 --> 36:32.610
I've guessed that s
of n is theta of n.

36:32.610 --> 36:36.000
In particular, if I plug into
this recursive relationship

36:36.000 --> 36:39.428
here, on the left-hand
side, I'm going to get cn.

36:39.428 --> 36:44.290
On the right-hand side, I'm
going to get c n minus 1

36:44.290 --> 36:47.800
plus theta of 1.

36:47.800 --> 36:51.080
We just have to make sure
that this is an OK equal sign.

36:51.080 --> 36:51.870
So what can I do?

36:51.870 --> 36:54.260
I can subtract cn
from both sides,

36:54.260 --> 36:56.580
maybe put that 1 on
the other side here.

36:56.580 --> 37:00.090
Then we get the c
equals big I of 1.

37:00.090 --> 37:01.432
c is, of course, a constant.

37:01.432 --> 37:02.390
So we're in good shape.

37:05.230 --> 37:06.850
My undergrad
algorithms professor

37:06.850 --> 37:09.880
told me never to write a victory
mark at the end of a proof.

37:09.880 --> 37:11.800
You have to do a little square.

37:11.800 --> 37:13.770
But he's not here.

37:16.650 --> 37:18.008
So now, I see you.

37:18.008 --> 37:19.300
But we're a little low on time.

37:19.300 --> 37:21.550
So we'll save it
for the lecture.

37:21.550 --> 37:22.050
OK.

37:22.050 --> 37:25.470
So if we want to implement the
selection sort algorithm, well,

37:25.470 --> 37:26.560
what do we do?

37:26.560 --> 37:30.420
Well, we're going to think of
i as the index of that red line

37:30.420 --> 37:32.100
that I was showing you before.

37:32.100 --> 37:35.620
Everything beyond i
is already sorted.

37:35.620 --> 37:37.990
So in selection sort, the
first thing I'm going to do

37:37.990 --> 37:41.110
is find the max element
between 0 and i.

37:41.110 --> 37:44.290
And then I'm going to
swap it into place.

37:44.290 --> 37:46.770
So this is just a code
version of the technique

37:46.770 --> 37:48.240
we've already talked about.

37:48.240 --> 37:49.590
Hopefully, this makes sense.

37:49.590 --> 37:53.310
So you find the biggest
element between 0 and index i.

37:53.310 --> 37:55.800
That's what we're
going to call j here.

37:55.800 --> 37:58.350
I swap that with
the one in index i.

37:58.350 --> 38:00.480
That's step 2.

38:00.480 --> 38:02.670
And then step 3 is I still
have to sort everything

38:02.670 --> 38:06.760
to the left of index i and
that's that recursive call.

38:06.760 --> 38:09.940
So if I want to
justify the runtime

38:09.940 --> 38:12.910
of this particular
technique, well, now

38:12.910 --> 38:15.110
let's call that t for time.

38:18.280 --> 38:19.310
Well, what do I do?

38:19.310 --> 38:23.350
Well, for one, I call selection
sort with index i minus 1.

38:23.350 --> 38:27.390
So that incurs time
that looks like this.

38:27.390 --> 38:30.942
But I also call that
prefix max function.

38:30.942 --> 38:32.370
And how much time
does that take?

38:32.370 --> 38:35.300
That takes order n time.

38:35.300 --> 38:40.135
So at the end of the day,
I have some relationship

38:40.135 --> 38:41.010
that looks like this.

38:41.010 --> 38:43.170
Does that makes sense?

38:43.170 --> 38:45.740
So by the way, notice that
this order n swallowed

38:45.740 --> 38:51.960
up the order 1 computations that
I had to do to swap and so on.

38:51.960 --> 38:56.190
So remember, there's this
nice relationship, which

38:56.190 --> 38:58.710
you probably learned in your
combinatorics class, which

38:58.710 --> 39:01.690
is that 1 plus 2 plus
dot, dot, dot plus n.

39:01.690 --> 39:02.190
OK.

39:02.190 --> 39:03.898
I can never remember
exactly the formula.

39:03.898 --> 39:07.980
But I'm pretty sure that
it looks like n squared.

39:07.980 --> 39:10.170
So based on that
and taking a look

39:10.170 --> 39:12.820
at this recursive thing, which
is essentially doing exactly

39:12.820 --> 39:13.320
that--

39:13.320 --> 39:16.140
n plus n minus 1 plus
n minus 2, and so on--

39:16.140 --> 39:19.950
I might hypothesize that
this thing is really

39:19.950 --> 39:22.587
order n squared.

39:22.587 --> 39:24.170
So if I'm going to
do that, then again

39:24.170 --> 39:26.510
if I want to use the
same technique for proof,

39:26.510 --> 39:29.300
I have to plug this
relationship in, and then double

39:29.300 --> 39:30.580
check that is consistent.

39:30.580 --> 39:37.400
So maybe I hypothesize that
t of n equals cn squared.

39:37.400 --> 39:41.400
In which case, I
plug it in here.

39:41.400 --> 39:45.500
I have cn squared equals
with a question mark over it

39:45.500 --> 39:52.552
cn minus 1 squared plus
big O or even theta n here.

39:52.552 --> 39:54.520
So if I expand
the square, notice

39:54.520 --> 39:57.150
I'm going to get
c times n squared

39:57.150 --> 39:59.610
plus a bunch of linear stuff.

39:59.610 --> 40:04.170
This is really cn squared--

40:04.170 --> 40:05.880
I should be careful with that--

40:05.880 --> 40:12.020
minus 2 cn plus c
plus theta of n.

40:14.575 --> 40:18.520
Notice that there's a cn squared
on both sides of this equation.

40:18.520 --> 40:19.700
They go away.

40:19.700 --> 40:23.410
And what I'm left with is
a nice, consistent formula

40:23.410 --> 40:32.320
that theta of n
equals 2 cn minus c.

40:32.320 --> 40:34.185
And indeed, this is
an order n expression.

40:34.185 --> 40:35.560
So there's order
in the universe.

40:35.560 --> 40:36.287
Life is good.

40:36.287 --> 40:37.870
Yeah, this is the
substitution method.

40:37.870 --> 40:41.085
And again, I think you'll cover
it more in your recitation.

40:41.085 --> 40:41.960
So what have we done?

40:41.960 --> 40:43.840
We have derived
the selection sort.

40:43.840 --> 40:46.810
We've checked that it
runs in n squared time.

40:46.810 --> 40:49.910
And by this nice,
inductive strategy,

40:49.910 --> 40:52.220
we know that it's correct.

40:52.220 --> 40:53.200
So life is pretty good.

40:53.200 --> 40:55.367
Unfortunately, I promised
for you guys on the slides

40:55.367 --> 40:57.130
that sorting really
takes n log n time.

40:57.130 --> 40:59.100
And this is an order
n squared algorithm.

40:59.100 --> 41:01.270
So we're not quite done yet.

41:01.270 --> 41:02.570
I'm way over time.

41:02.570 --> 41:05.470
So we're going to skip a
different algorithm, which

41:05.470 --> 41:07.750
is called insertion sort,
also runs on n time.

41:10.890 --> 41:13.140
Essentially, insertion sort
runs in the reverse order.

41:13.140 --> 41:14.848
I'm going to sort
everything to the left,

41:14.848 --> 41:17.433
and then insert a new object,
whereas, in selection, I'm

41:17.433 --> 41:18.850
going to choose
the biggest object

41:18.850 --> 41:20.858
and then sort
everything to the left.

41:20.858 --> 41:22.900
But I'll let you guys
piece through that at home.

41:22.900 --> 41:24.640
It's essentially
the same argument.

41:24.640 --> 41:27.460
And instead, we should
jump to an algorithm

41:27.460 --> 41:31.150
that actually matters, which
is something called merge sort.

41:31.150 --> 41:34.300
How many of us have
encountered merge sort before?

41:34.300 --> 41:34.880
Fabulous.

41:34.880 --> 41:35.380
Good.

41:35.380 --> 41:37.810
So then I'm done.

41:37.810 --> 41:39.342
So let's say that I have a list.

41:39.342 --> 41:41.050
Now, I'm sending a
message back to Jason.

41:41.050 --> 41:42.640
I made this one up last night.

41:42.640 --> 41:45.820
So I have 7, 1,
5, 6, 2, 4, 9, 3.

41:45.820 --> 41:47.980
This is not in sorted order.

41:47.980 --> 41:50.090
But I can make a very
deep observation,

41:50.090 --> 41:52.690
which is that every number
by itself is in sorted order

41:52.690 --> 41:56.380
if I think of it as
an array of length 1.

41:56.380 --> 42:00.670
It's really deep, like
deep learning deep.

42:00.670 --> 42:02.240
So now, what can I do?

42:02.240 --> 42:06.167
Well, I could take every pair of
numbers, draw a little red box.

42:06.167 --> 42:07.750
Well, now, they're
not in sorted order

42:07.750 --> 42:09.230
any more inside
of the red boxes.

42:09.230 --> 42:10.972
So I'm going to sort
inside of every box.

42:10.972 --> 42:12.430
In this case, it's
not too exciting

42:12.430 --> 42:14.560
because it's just pairs.

42:14.560 --> 42:17.350
And now, they're in sorted order
because they said they were.

42:17.350 --> 42:19.570
Now, I'm going to keep
doubling the size of my boxes.

42:19.570 --> 42:22.600
So now, let's say I
have box of length 4.

42:22.600 --> 42:25.300
What do I know about the
left and right-hand sides

42:25.300 --> 42:28.230
of the dotted lines here?

42:28.230 --> 42:30.750
On the two sides of
the dotted lines,

42:30.750 --> 42:32.400
the array is in sorted order.

42:32.400 --> 42:33.585
There's a 1 and then a 7.

42:33.585 --> 42:35.800
Those are in sorted
order, 5 and a 6.

42:35.800 --> 42:39.650
That's because, in the previous
step, I sorted every pair.

42:39.650 --> 42:43.580
So when I merge these
two sides together,

42:43.580 --> 42:46.700
I have an additional useful
piece of information,

42:46.700 --> 42:49.460
namely that the two
sides of the dotted line

42:49.460 --> 42:50.690
are already in sorted order.

42:50.690 --> 42:54.530
That's going to be our
basic inductive step here.

42:54.530 --> 42:56.520
So in this case, I
merge the two sides.

42:56.520 --> 42:59.010
I get 1, 5, 6, 7,
and 2, 3, 4, 9.

42:59.010 --> 43:01.380
Then finally, I put these
two things together.

43:01.380 --> 43:03.650
And I have to sort these two.

43:03.650 --> 43:06.380
I have to merge these
two sorted lists.

43:06.380 --> 43:08.180
But they're in sorted order.

43:08.180 --> 43:11.290
And that's going to give me a
big advantage because-- oops,

43:11.290 --> 43:15.080
I lost my chalk.

43:15.080 --> 43:18.770
I suppose I've got space
on this board here.

43:18.770 --> 43:21.050
Oh no.

43:21.050 --> 43:29.666
So if I want to merge 1,
5, 6, 7 and 2, 3, 4, 9,

43:29.666 --> 43:31.430
there's a nice, clever
technique that we

43:31.430 --> 43:34.305
can do that's going to
take just linear time.

43:34.305 --> 43:36.180
Jason tells me it's the
two finger algorithm.

43:36.180 --> 43:37.715
I think that's a
cute analogy here.

43:37.715 --> 43:38.840
So here are my two fingers.

43:38.840 --> 43:40.790
They're going to point
at the end of the list.

43:40.790 --> 43:44.150
And I'm going to construct
the merged array backwards.

43:44.150 --> 43:46.610
So how many elements are
in my merged array, if I'm

43:46.610 --> 43:49.400
merging two things of length 4?

43:49.400 --> 43:52.020
I don't ask you
guys hard questions.

43:52.020 --> 43:52.920
It's 8, yeah?

43:52.920 --> 43:54.076
4 plus 4.

43:54.076 --> 43:55.920
8, yeah?

43:55.920 --> 43:56.930
So what do I know?

43:56.930 --> 44:00.000
I know that my merge array--

44:00.000 --> 44:04.240
5, 6, 7-- has eight elements.

44:04.240 --> 44:07.800
And now, I'm going to have two
fingers at the end of my array.

44:07.800 --> 44:11.490
Which one should I put at
the end of the merged guy?

44:11.490 --> 44:12.390
The 7 of the 9?

44:15.318 --> 44:16.300
AUDIENCE: The 9

44:16.300 --> 44:17.010
JUSTIN: The 9.

44:17.010 --> 44:19.040
Right, thank you.

44:19.040 --> 44:23.737
So now, I can move my
lower finger to the left

44:23.737 --> 44:25.070
because I've already added that.

44:25.070 --> 44:27.278
Notice that I never need to
look to the left of where

44:27.278 --> 44:30.020
my finger is because they're
already in sorted order.

44:30.020 --> 44:31.993
Now what should I
add, the 4 or the 7?

44:31.993 --> 44:32.820
AUDIENCE: 7.

44:32.820 --> 44:35.150
JUSTIN: The 7.

44:35.150 --> 44:37.888
And so on, dot, dot, dot, yeah?

44:37.888 --> 44:40.180
So that's going to be the
basic idea of the merge sort.

44:40.180 --> 44:41.920
I'm going to take
two sorted lists.

44:41.920 --> 44:43.837
And I'm going to make a
new sorted list, which

44:43.837 --> 44:46.210
is twice as long,
by using two fingers

44:46.210 --> 44:49.530
and moving from
the and backward.

44:49.530 --> 44:51.720
So that's the basic
intuition here.

44:51.720 --> 44:53.220
Indeed, there's our sorted list.

44:53.220 --> 44:55.440
It's stressing me out
that there's no eight.

44:55.440 --> 44:58.500
I need the power of 2.

44:58.500 --> 45:00.930
So I think merge sort,
we're going to present it

45:00.930 --> 45:02.790
in a backward way
from the previous one,

45:02.790 --> 45:04.620
where I'm going to give you
the high level algorithm.

45:04.620 --> 45:06.670
And then actually, the
headache is that merging step,

45:06.670 --> 45:07.920
which I have four minutes for.

45:07.920 --> 45:10.000
And I apologize for it.

45:10.000 --> 45:11.340
So what does the merger sort do?

45:11.340 --> 45:13.200
Well, it computes
an index c, which

45:13.200 --> 45:15.142
is the middle of my array.

45:15.142 --> 45:17.350
And it's going to make a
recursive call which is sort

45:17.350 --> 45:22.423
the left, which is everything
between index A and index C.

45:22.423 --> 45:24.840
And then sort everything on
the right, which is everything

45:24.840 --> 45:28.620
from index C to index B.
I know this is confusing

45:28.620 --> 45:30.840
because usually letters
appear in order.

45:30.840 --> 45:33.600
But C, if you think of
as standing for center,

45:33.600 --> 45:34.650
then it makes sense like.

45:34.650 --> 45:35.340
Here's my array.

45:38.310 --> 45:40.530
I'm going to choose an
index right in the middle.

45:40.530 --> 45:44.190
I've done myself a disservice
by not using a power of 2.

45:44.190 --> 45:45.360
But that's OK.

45:45.360 --> 45:48.870
I'm going to say sort everything
to the left of the dotted line

45:48.870 --> 45:49.810
first.

45:49.810 --> 45:52.200
Sort everything to the right
of the dotted line second.

45:52.200 --> 45:54.930
Now, I have two sorted
lists on the two

45:54.930 --> 45:56.040
sides of the dotted line.

45:56.040 --> 45:59.392
And then I'm going to use my two
fingers to put them together.

45:59.392 --> 46:01.100
So that's what this
is implementing here.

46:01.100 --> 46:03.080
See, there's two
recursive calls--

46:03.080 --> 46:05.402
sort from A to C, and then
sort from C to B. Oops,

46:05.402 --> 46:06.610
I didn't actually label this.

46:06.610 --> 46:11.870
So this is A, C, B. And
then I've got to call merge.

46:16.130 --> 46:18.890
Now, our implementation
of merge--

46:18.890 --> 46:21.440
well, we can also do this
in a recursive fashion.

46:21.440 --> 46:23.640
But personally, I find
this a little complicated.

46:23.640 --> 46:24.590
I'm going to admit.

46:24.590 --> 46:28.700
But here's the basic idea
here, which I'm now rushing.

46:31.830 --> 46:35.820
So I'm going to think of
my upper finger as finger i

46:35.820 --> 46:38.060
and my lower finger as finger j.

46:38.060 --> 46:40.320
Does that makes sense?

46:40.320 --> 46:43.830
So I have two sorted lists.

46:43.830 --> 46:46.380
So maybe like that.

46:46.380 --> 46:49.350
I don't know, 1, 3, 5, 7.

46:49.350 --> 46:52.240
And then I have a
second sorted list here,

46:52.240 --> 46:58.950
which is maybe 2, 4,
6, 72, as one does.

46:58.950 --> 47:03.900
Then I'm going to have one
pointer like this, which is i,

47:03.900 --> 47:06.970
and a pointer down
here, which is j.

47:06.970 --> 47:13.526
And my goal is to
construct an array A

47:13.526 --> 47:15.960
with a bunch of elements in it.

47:15.960 --> 47:18.200
And the way that I'm
going to do it is I'm

47:18.200 --> 47:21.440
going to use exactly the same
kind of recursive argument,

47:21.440 --> 47:24.950
that I can either have
the biggest element of my

47:24.950 --> 47:27.050
be the last element
of the first guy

47:27.050 --> 47:30.890
or be the last element
of the second one.

47:30.890 --> 47:33.012
So here's going to be
our recursive call.

47:33.012 --> 47:34.970
And in addition to that,
for convenience, we'll

47:34.970 --> 47:39.710
have a third index, which is B,
which is pointing to this thing

47:39.710 --> 47:43.710
inside of my sorted array that
I'm currently processing Yeah?

47:43.710 --> 47:46.112
It's going to start
at A, go to B.

47:46.112 --> 47:47.570
Incidentally, I
see a lot of people

47:47.570 --> 47:48.830
taking photos of the slides.

47:48.830 --> 47:51.980
These are just copy
pasted from the notes.

47:51.980 --> 47:52.790
OK.

47:52.790 --> 47:57.960
So in this case, what should
I put in B for my two arrays?

47:57.960 --> 48:02.340
I have 1, 3, 5, 7; 2, 4, 6, 72.

48:02.340 --> 48:05.170
72, yeah?

48:05.170 --> 48:05.770
Great.

48:05.770 --> 48:07.070
So now, what am I going to do?

48:07.070 --> 48:10.180
I'm just going to call
the merge function.

48:10.180 --> 48:14.340
But I'm going to decrement
B because now I'm

48:14.340 --> 48:15.960
happy with that last element.

48:15.960 --> 48:19.620
And in addition to that,
I'm going to decrement j

48:19.620 --> 48:22.710
because I already used it up.

48:22.710 --> 48:24.900
And so that's our
recursive call here.

48:24.900 --> 48:27.270
It's saying, if j is
less than or equal to 0--

48:27.270 --> 48:28.770
so in other words,
I have an element

48:28.770 --> 48:31.830
to use in one of the
lists of the other.

48:31.830 --> 48:36.223
And maybe the left one is
bigger than the right one.

48:36.223 --> 48:37.140
That's our first case.

48:37.140 --> 48:39.630
That does not apply
in this example here.

48:39.630 --> 48:41.400
Well, then I should
make the last element

48:41.400 --> 48:44.520
of a from the first
list and then recurse

48:44.520 --> 48:48.210
with one fewer element
i, and similarly

48:48.210 --> 48:50.580
the reverse case for j.

48:50.580 --> 48:53.760
So if we do our runtime
in two minutes or less--

48:53.760 --> 48:56.190
bare with me guys--

48:56.190 --> 48:59.580
well, what is this merge
function going to do?

48:59.580 --> 49:02.700
Well, in some sense,
there's two branches.

49:02.700 --> 49:04.380
There's an if statement
with two pieces.

49:04.380 --> 49:07.350
But both of those
pieces call merge

49:07.350 --> 49:10.520
with one fewer piece in it.

49:10.520 --> 49:15.470
So in some sense, we have s
of n equals s of n minus 1

49:15.470 --> 49:18.680
plus theta of 1,
which we already

49:18.680 --> 49:24.230
know from our previous
proof means that s of n

49:24.230 --> 49:27.870
is equal to theta of n.

49:27.870 --> 49:30.972
So in other words, it
takes linear time to merge.

49:30.972 --> 49:33.180
It makes sense intuitively
because essentially you're

49:33.180 --> 49:34.597
touching every one
of these things

49:34.597 --> 49:38.440
once with your two fingers.

49:38.440 --> 49:40.660
And now, probably
the hardest part

49:40.660 --> 49:42.700
of the lecture, which
I left zero time for,

49:42.700 --> 49:47.300
is deriving the runtime for the
actual merge sort algorithm.

49:47.300 --> 49:48.680
And what does that look like?

49:48.680 --> 49:52.870
Well, that one's a little bit
trickier because, of course,

49:52.870 --> 49:56.290
I call the merge sort
algorithm twice, each time

49:56.290 --> 49:58.240
on a list that's half the size.

49:58.240 --> 50:00.740
In this class, we're going to
assume that our list is always

50:00.740 --> 50:02.360
a power of 2 in its length.

50:02.360 --> 50:06.100
Otherwise, this analysis
is a itty bitty bit more

50:06.100 --> 50:07.190
of a headache.

50:07.190 --> 50:09.190
So first of all,
how long does it

50:09.190 --> 50:11.780
take to sort an
array of length 1?

50:11.780 --> 50:14.600
I am not going to
ask hard questions.

50:14.600 --> 50:16.750
Everybody?

50:16.750 --> 50:19.060
Yeah, it's just 1, right?

50:19.060 --> 50:20.835
Because there's nothing to do.

50:20.835 --> 50:23.620
An array of length 1 has
one element and it's sorted.

50:23.620 --> 50:28.540
It's also the biggest element
and the smallest element.

50:28.540 --> 50:31.000
And now, what does
our algorithm do?

50:31.000 --> 50:33.370
Well, it makes two
recursive calls

50:33.370 --> 50:34.810
on lists that are
half the length.

50:40.290 --> 50:42.040
And then it calls
that merge function.

50:42.040 --> 50:46.858
And we know that the merge
function takes theta of n time.

50:46.858 --> 50:50.150
Does that make sense?

50:50.150 --> 50:53.200
So one thing we might do,
because we have some intuition

50:53.200 --> 50:56.470
from your 6042
course, is that we

50:56.470 --> 51:06.990
think that this thing is order
n log n because it makes the two

51:06.990 --> 51:07.708
recursive calls.

51:07.708 --> 51:09.000
And then it puts them together.

51:09.000 --> 51:11.417
And let's double check that
that's true really quick using

51:11.417 --> 51:12.670
the substitution method.

51:12.670 --> 51:14.700
So in particular, on
the left-hand side

51:14.700 --> 51:18.990
here, maybe I have cn log n.

51:18.990 --> 51:21.420
Now, I have 2 c.

51:21.420 --> 51:29.250
Well, I have to put an n over
2 log n over 2 plus theta of n.

51:29.250 --> 51:32.740
And I want to double check that
this expression is consistent.

51:32.740 --> 51:36.330
I've got about a
foot to do it in.

51:36.330 --> 51:37.920
So remember-- let's see.

51:37.920 --> 51:42.690
If we use our favorite
identities from high school

51:42.690 --> 51:44.760
class that you probably
forgot, remember

51:44.760 --> 51:46.890
that log of 2 things
divided by each other

51:46.890 --> 51:48.720
is the difference of the logs.

51:48.720 --> 51:50.830
So this is really 2.

51:50.830 --> 51:51.330
OK.

51:51.330 --> 51:53.310
2 divided by 2 is 1.

51:53.310 --> 52:05.900
So this is c times n times log
n minus log of 2 plus theta n.

52:05.900 --> 52:07.290
I'm already out of time.

52:07.290 --> 52:12.290
But notice that there's a c n
log n on the right-hand side.

52:12.290 --> 52:15.360
There's a c n log n
on the left-hand side.

52:15.360 --> 52:17.760
So those two things go away.

52:17.760 --> 52:19.500
And what am I going
to be left with?

52:19.500 --> 52:29.630
I'm going to be left with
theta of n equals cn log of 2.

52:29.630 --> 52:32.547
Notice that c and log
2 are both constants.

52:32.547 --> 52:34.380
We have a theta event
on the left-hand side.

52:34.380 --> 52:35.755
So there's order
in the universe.

52:35.755 --> 52:38.120
And we've derived our runtime.

52:38.120 --> 52:40.310
So I know I rest a little
bit through merge sort.

52:40.310 --> 52:42.890
I'm sure that Erik and Jason
can review this a little bit

52:42.890 --> 52:44.000
next time.

52:44.000 --> 52:46.970
But with that,
we'll see you, what?

52:46.970 --> 52:48.140
Thursday and Friday.

52:48.140 --> 52:51.100
And it's been a pleasure
to talk to you all.
